 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over WebSocket connections the board operates itself: delivery pushes each committed revision to every connected browser session, so updates arrive with push latency rather than on a polling cadence.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Operating the realtime path ourselves puts three obligations on the design: the change store must publish each committed revision reliably and replay a revision range; delivery must own WebSocket session capacity, per-session ordering, and reconnection; and browsers must detect dropped connections, reconnect, and catch up on revisions missed while disconnected. This connection and reconnect operations work is accepted in exchange for fast updates with no realtime vendor dependency.
 
